        "section": [
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Aortic regurgitation, managed medically or surgically</div>"
            },
            "code": {
              "coding": [
                {
                  "code": "67781-5",
                  "system": "http://loinc.org",
                  "display": "Summarization of encounter note"
                }
              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the recent hospital admission of your 74-year-old male patient, who presented with symptomatic aortic valve regurgitation. On admission, he reported progressive exertional dyspnoea and mild peripheral oedema. Clinical examination revealed a characteristic early diastolic murmur. Transthoracic echocardiography confirmed significant aortic regurgitation with preserved left ventricular function. An ECG and chest X-ray were performed, alongside routine blood tests. Following cardiology review and multidisciplinary discussion, the patient was deemed unsuitable for surgical intervention due to his comorbidity profile and age. He was therefore managed medically with optimised vasodilator therapy and diuretics. His symptoms improved satisfactorily during the short admission. He has been discharged with outpatient cardiology follow-up arranged. Please continue monitoring fluid status and blood pressure. Do not hesitate to contact me should you require further information.</div>"
            },
            "code": {
